WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:18.350
In the 
first two years of World War II the Allies

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:23.530
were stunned by the speed, coordination and
efficacy of German’s armoured divisions,

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:24.660
seemingly unstoppable.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.870
It was the success of the Blitzkrieg tactics,
the ‘lightning war’.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:34.940
This success was attributed to the leadership
and military genius of today’s protagonist.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:39.579
Even before the war, his published works had
brought him to the attention of military leaders

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:40.579
worldwide.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:43.180
He became known as ‘The Father of Blitzkrieg’.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:48.320
His loyalty to Germany was undisputed, but
his support of Hitler and Nazi leadership

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.899
was not so clear cut.

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:54.989
He was in fact one of the very few Generals
who dared shouting back at the Fuhrer and

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:56.510
even disobey his orders.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:02.990
Please join me today for the story of General
Heinz Guderian.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:10.610
Some clarifications on Blitzkrieg tactics
Before we dive bomb like a Stuka into Heinz

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:16.210
Guderian’s life, I will bore you with some
military theory, as we need to clarify some

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.210
concepts.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:21.490
Blitzkrieg tactics can be defined as the use
of combined forces of tanks, motorised infantry

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:27.409
and artillery, breaking the opponent’s defences
on a narrow front, bypassing pockets of resistance

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:30.240
and striking deep into enemy territory.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.479
Ground forces are provided close support by
the air force, tasked with bombing key objectives

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:36.950
and establishing local air superiority.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.650
The term ‘blitzkrieg’, was actually coined
by the British press, not by the German high

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:46.470
command, as a means to describe the swift
invasion of Poland in September 1939.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:54.670
German military would have used the term ‘Bewegungskrieg’,
or “War of movement”, and it is a military

00:01:54.670 --> 00:02:00.120
doctrine, rather than a tactic.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:06.080
A tactic is a method of employing forces in
combat, or the ‘how’ you can win a battle.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.729
Military doctrine is the set of principles
by which Armed Forces are organised.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:15.300
Doctrine shapes strategies, which are the
plans to achieve victory in a war.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.080
I hope I got that right, if any of you have
served, or are serving in the military, please

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.099
feel free to correct me in the comments.

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:27.630
But going back to Bewegungskrieg, this doctrine
had been shaping the German armed forces’

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:32.950
plans since WWI. Heinz Guderian and other
military theorists after him, perfected the

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:38.140
doctrine and took advantage of current technology
to maximise the potential of its theoretical

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.140
principles.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.360
So, rather than calling Guderian ‘the father
of Blitzkrieg’, he should be ‘the optimiser

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.360
of Bewegungskrieg’.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.670
Not as catchy, I know.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:52.819
But with that out of the way, let’s begin
with the life of Heinz.

00:02:52.819 --> 00:03:00.810
Heinz(el) and Gretel
Heinz Guderian was born on the 7th of June,

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.680
1888 in Kulm, Prussia, now in Polish territory.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.709
He came to life into a close-knit family with
a long-standing military tradition.

00:03:08.709 --> 00:03:12.739
His father, Friedrich, had been a colonel
in the Prussian, then German army.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:17.129
Heinz followed his father’s career enthusiastically
and as teenager enlisted in the Principal

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:18.700
Cadet School in Berlin.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:23.629
He was an ambitious and determined student,
his teachers described him as

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:26.320
'always serious'
Well, not always.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.290
Sometimes he was described as
'very serious'.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.940
Heinz soon gained a useful leadership skill,
that is the ability of speaking very concisely

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.739
and clearly to communicate his ideas, and
later his orders.

00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:41.560
At will, he could turn his communication style
into one that was cold and hurting.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.410
This made him equally famous and feared at
Cadet School, but not very popular.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.450
It appears he made no friends there, at least
until he graduated and got his first command

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:51.450
post.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:56.610
In 1907, aged 19, Heinz was sent to join the
Tenth Battalion as 2nd Lieutenant under the

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:57.610
command of his father.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:01.980
We don’t know if this was by design, but
we do know that Heinz did not take advantage

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.780
of the situation to just slouch around.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:09.350
Friedrich Guderian was a stern leader, both
loved and feared by his soldiers and his family,

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.290
and he would not make any favours to his son.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.920
Heinz worked hard and learned a great deal
from his father.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.789
Two years later Heinz Guderian’s unit was
transferred to the Goslar barracks in the

00:04:19.789 --> 00:04:22.870
Harz Mountains, one of the most scenic parts
of Germany.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:27.719
He had some time to go horse riding, hunting
and socialising, and so he met and fell in

00:04:27.719 --> 00:04:31.059
love with Margarete Goerne, whom he nicknamed
Gretel.

00:04:31.059 --> 00:04:35.310
Gretel was the perfect companion for Heinz,
as she shared and supported his ambitious

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:36.310
outlook on life.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:40.689
They would marry in 1913, and Heinz later
described her as the perfect soldier’s wife:

00:04:40.689 --> 00:04:45.279
she could be cool and sensible when needed,
most of all she was able to calm his husband

00:04:45.279 --> 00:04:48.279
when seized by frequent fits of anger.

00:04:48.279 --> 00:04:51.939
Very often in his later life Guderian’s
hot temper got him into trouble for lashing

00:04:51.939 --> 00:04:54.439
out at subalterns and superiors alike.

00:04:54.439 --> 00:04:58.850
On many occasions he had to be saved from
disciplinary measures by a superior who appreciated

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:02.740
his exceptional military ability, despite
a poor impulse control.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:06.199
Later in life Guderian took snap decisions
to disobey orders from above.

00:05:06.199 --> 00:05:12.059
He clearly had a talent for leading, he just
couldn’t be bothered with following.

00:05:12.059 --> 00:05:19.120
Schnelle Heinz
The year before his wedding, 1912, Heinz wanted

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.689
to broaden his technical training and so he
transferred to the Third Telegraph Battalion.

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:27.490
Here he studied hard to become a specialist
in the new radio-signal equipment.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:31.499
Already at this stage, he saw communications
as a decisive element for military victory.

00:05:31.499 --> 00:05:35.569
His studies were not limited to radio: he
studied military strategy, French and English,

00:05:35.569 --> 00:05:39.039
so he could study military theory text books
in those languages.

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:44.289
His efforts were noticed, and Heinz was offered
to join a class of 168 officers to attend

00:05:44.289 --> 00:05:46.680
a three-year program at the War Academy in
Berlin.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.860
He was moving fast, so much so that he earned
the nickname ‘Schnelle Heinz’, or ‘Quick

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:52.860
Heinz’.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.969
Although he did not see himself as necessarily
‘quick’.

00:05:54.969 --> 00:05:59.610
Heinz did not like to rush mindlessly into
action, often quoting a line by military leader

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:03.719
Helmuth von Moltke:
"First reckon, then risk,"

00:06:03.719 --> 00:06:08.210
Heinz Guderian's studies were interrupted
when two gunshots rang out in Sarajevo on

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:15.509
the 28th of June 1914: the assassination of
Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary

00:06:15.509 --> 00:06:18.990
led to the outbreak of World War I in a matter
of weeks.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.770
With his radio qualification, Guderian joined
the wireless communication staff.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:28.199
In November of 1914, he was promoted to 1st
Lieutenant and only one year later to Captain.

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:32.569
His work allowed him to witness almost every
front of the war, including the disaster on

00:06:32.569 --> 00:06:34.360
the Marne and the slaughter of Verdun.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.120
The carnage he viewed may have influenced
his thoughts on developing mechanized warfare

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:41.309
to protect the infantrymen and achieve quick
victory.

00:06:41.309 --> 00:06:45.490
He never had the chance to command a fighting
unit, but his radio station sometimes came

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:48.050
under attack and he had to fight his way out.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.819
As a result, he was awarded the Iron Cross
second and first class.

00:06:51.819 --> 00:06:56.059
In early 1918, he was tested at an advanced
officer training programme, where he showed

00:06:56.059 --> 00:06:59.679
the ability for solving tactical problems
with unusual actions.

00:06:59.679 --> 00:07:03.740
This impressed his instructors, which accepted
his request to join the General Staff of the

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:04.819
Army High Command.

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:10.399
It is evident how the WWI experiences shaped
Guderian’s thoughts on Bewegungskrieg and

00:07:10.399 --> 00:07:11.539
how to improve it.

00:07:11.539 --> 00:07:15.979
His post in charge of radio communications
taught him the importance of the wireless,

00:07:15.979 --> 00:07:20.559
especially when it came to coordinating the
movements of separate across infantry, artillery

00:07:20.559 --> 00:07:21.939
or cavalry.

00:07:21.939 --> 00:07:26.559
Although not directly involved, Guderian studied
the tactics employed by the Stosstruppen,

00:07:26.559 --> 00:07:29.659
also known as ‘Shock troops’ or ‘Storm
troopers’.

00:07:29.659 --> 00:07:34.969
These units we usually successful in infiltrating
weak spots of enemy trenches to encircle,

00:07:34.969 --> 00:07:36.759
capture or destroy command centres.

00:07:36.759 --> 00:07:42.050
But their successes were short-lived because
of the limitations of, well, just being human.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:46.159
The extent of the advance of shock troops
was limited by how fast and how far they could

00:07:46.159 --> 00:07:48.190
walk before succumbing to exhaustion.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:53.000
In many cases, capturing a well-stocked British
or French position would mean failure, as

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.999
shock troops just sat down to eat.

00:07:54.999 --> 00:07:59.210
Guderian’s theories proposed to solve these
limits by supporting infantry with mechanized

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:00.599
and motorised units.

00:08:00.599 --> 00:08:05.520
They would require in turn the integration
and coordination of separate arms at the lowest

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.629
unit level, in mutually supportive functions.

00:08:08.629 --> 00:08:13.300
In lay terms – if you have a platoon of
infantry - 30-50 men - fighting alongside

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:17.849
a tank platoon – 4 or 6 tanks – and they
support each other, it is easier for them

00:08:17.849 --> 00:08:22.659
to cooperate, rather than having a whole division
of 15,000 foot soldiers on one side, and a

00:08:22.659 --> 00:08:24.800
division of tanks on the other.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.180
Again, my military friends, correct me if
I am wrong.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:37.090
War of Movement 2.0[TA1]
When the war ended in German defeat, the Treaty

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:41.490
of Versailles allowed the Country to maintain
only a small army of 100,000 troops, with

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.080
no tanks nor aircraft.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:47.190
Post-war, Guderian served as an instructor,
teaching tactics and military history.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:52.019
It was during these years that he expanded,
developed and perfected his take on the ideal

00:08:52.019 --> 00:08:54.020
War of Movement.

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:58.380
He would digest the works on mechanized warfare
of British theorists Captain Liddell Hart

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.000
and Major-General Fuller.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.139
He began publishing articles in military journals
and won a reputation for clear thinking on

00:09:05.139 --> 00:09:06.319
controversial matters.

00:09:06.319 --> 00:09:09.180
In 1927, he was promoted to the rank of Major.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:13.589
His role was at the head of the Command for
all motorized transport units, and he also

00:09:13.589 --> 00:09:16.430
taught transport tactics at the Berlin military
academy.

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:21.060
Basically, his units were tasked with logistics
and transport, not actual combat.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.839
But this did not prevent him from taking advantage
and play around with his trucks.

00:09:25.839 --> 00:09:29.639
He equipped some of these vehicles with wooden
turrets, armed with guns, and he moved them

00:09:29.639 --> 00:09:31.899
around a training ground as fighting units.

00:09:31.899 --> 00:09:34.839
His superiors were not happy and put an end
to his experiments.

00:09:34.839 --> 00:09:39.150
But what he could not learn at home, he would
learn abroad.

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:44.089
In 1929, Guderian travelled to Sweden, where
he visited a tank battalion, then to Kazan,

00:09:44.089 --> 00:09:45.089
Russia.

00:09:45.089 --> 00:09:49.420
Here he visited a secret tank testing facility
co-developed by the Weimar Republic and the

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:50.420
Soviet Government.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:54.220
Probably he met Soviet tank commanders who
would later become his deadly enemies.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:10:00.160
By 1933, when the National Socialist Party
rose to power, Heinz was Colonel Guderian.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:04.300
Guderian continued with his articles about
armoured and motorized warfare and helped

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:09.959
with solving technical problems of the first
Panzer specimens.

00:10:09.959 --> 00:10:19.899
Guderian’ s theories would find fertile
ground with the Nazis and their rearmament

00:10:19.899 --> 00:10:20.899
policies.

00:10:20.899 --> 00:10:24.920
In early 1934, Guderian organised a demonstration
of Panzer tactics for Hitler.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:28.440
He later recalled
“The Chancellor was much taken by the speed

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:34.350
and precision of movement of our units and
said repeatedly ‘That’s what I need’!”

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:38.279
Hitler was ecstatic and would provide support
to Guderian’ s innovation, even though the

00:10:38.279 --> 00:10:42.010
two men often clashed in spectacular shouting
matches.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:46.540
Nazi leadership proceeded to re-install conscription,
thus ignoring the treaty of Versailles.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:50.360
Hitler himself authorized another violation
of the treaty, that is the creation of three

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:51.899
Panzer Divisions.

00:10:51.899 --> 00:10:56.380
Guderian was made the Commander of the 2nd
Panzer Division and shortly after was promoted

00:10:56.380 --> 00:11:01.329
to Major General, then Lieutenant General
in charge of the XVI Army-Corps.

00:11:01.329 --> 00:11:06.720
In 1938 Guderian’s units were the spearhead
of the Anschluss with Austria and the invasion

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.410
of the Sudetenland, the German-speaking region
of Czechoslovakia.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:14.910
These were the years of Appeasement, so the
guns on his tanks were still silent.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:20.050
A few months later, Guderian was promoted
to Commander in Chief of all the Fast Troops,

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:24.709
or Schnellen Truppen – the perfect job for
Schnelle Heinz!

00:11:24.709 --> 00:11:29.899
He was now in charge for the recruitment,
training, tactics and technique of all motorized

00:11:29.899 --> 00:11:30.899
and armoured units.

00:11:30.899 --> 00:11:35.040
A great leader and poor follower, now Guderian
would be able to bring the full weight of

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:39.509
his studies, doctrine and personality to the
German Panzer Divisions.

00:11:39.509 --> 00:11:43.519
He could finally formalise and implement his
take on the Bewegungskrieg.

00:11:43.519 --> 00:11:44.519
Which is:

00:11:44.519 --> 00:11:45.519
Nr one!

00:11:45.519 --> 00:11:48.399
Attacks should be led by large concentrated
number of tanks.

00:11:48.399 --> 00:11:52.149
Tanks should not be dispersed amongst other
units, like it was common practice in the

00:11:52.149 --> 00:11:54.389
British and French armies at that time.

00:11:54.389 --> 00:11:57.839
As Guderian put it:
“You hit somebody with your fist and not

00:11:57.839 --> 00:12:00.079
with your fingers spread”
Nr two!

00:12:00.079 --> 00:12:04.560
The infantry, transported by trucks and half-tracks,
should closely follow the tank platoons to

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:05.560
support them.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:09.769
This involves maintaining the logistics train,
occupying positions, taking prisoners, neutralising

00:12:09.769 --> 00:12:11.060
anti-tank weapons.

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:12.300
Nr three!

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:17.129
Aircraft and artillery are intended to support
both tanks and infantry, by striking at enemy

00:12:17.129 --> 00:12:20.570
armour and fortifications from above and from
afar.

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:21.570
Nr four!

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:24.779
The units involved require a reliable means
of communication.

00:12:24.779 --> 00:12:29.189
That involves connecting tanks via radio transmitters
for coordinated joint action.

00:12:29.189 --> 00:12:33.459
To generate public support for the Bewegungskrieg,
Guderian wrote and published his first book:

00:12:33.459 --> 00:12:34.459
‘Achtung!

00:12:34.459 --> 00:12:35.459
Panzer!’

00:12:35.459 --> 00:12:38.560
That’s how you pronounce it by the way.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:41.019
It’s not just “Achtung, Panzer”.

00:12:41.019 --> 00:12:42.019
It’s “Achtung!!”

00:12:42.019 --> 00:12:44.480
“Panzer!!!!”[TA2]
The book became a military bestseller, essential

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:46.069
reading for any officer.

00:12:46.069 --> 00:12:49.660
This book cemented Guderian’s reputation
as ‘Father of the Blitzkrieg’.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:53.320
Although it should be stressed again that
these were not original ideas – he simply

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:59.200
had perfected and formalised concepts borrowed
by German and foreign military strategists.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:04.110
An obscure French officer, a certain Charles
De Gaulle, was already a proponent of mechanized

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:07.879
war of movement in the 1920s, inspired by
Polish officer Sikorski.

00:13:07.879 --> 00:13:12.230
But in the end, what counts is not who theorises,
but who puts things into practice.

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:17.439
In the book, Guderian summed up this thinking:
"Deeds are more important than words.

00:13:17.439 --> 00:13:22.339
The goddess of battle will crown only the
most daring with laurels."

00:13:22.339 --> 00:13:29.339
Bewegungskrieg in practice
In September 1939 Schnelle Heinz was able

00:13:29.339 --> 00:13:33.769
to put his words into action, during the aggression
of Poland.

00:13:33.769 --> 00:13:39.160
Guderian was assigned the XIX Corps, consisting
of one panzer division and two motorized infantry

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.899
divisions which he led in an impressive drive
against Polish defences.

00:13:42.899 --> 00:13:46.529
But the real revolutionary actions took place
during the Battle of France in the following

00:13:46.529 --> 00:13:51.420
year, when the Wehrmacht concentrated their
armoured division and Luftwaffe at the Schwerpunkt,

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:55.050
or “main point of effort,” achieving a
decisive breakthrough against the Allies.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:59.350
The original plan for the invasion of France
was a repeat of the Schlieffen plan used in

00:13:59.350 --> 00:15:46.160
World War I: a quick attack through Belgium
into Northern France.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:49.759
But Hitler was not convinced:
“You won’t get away with an operation

00:15:49.759 --> 00:15:54.450
like that twice running”
Hitler demanded a new approach, based on secrecy

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:55.450
and surprise.

00:15:55.450 --> 00:16:00.449
The result was Operation Sickle Cut, devised
by General Von Manstein and Guderian.

00:16:00.449 --> 00:16:05.740
The idea was to lure French and British forces
into Belgium and Holland by launching decoy

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:10.059
attacks there, while the main thrust would
be advancing through the Ardennes forest,

00:16:10.059 --> 00:16:12.759
considered too thickly wooded for tank operations.

00:16:12.759 --> 00:16:16.490
The success of this plan depended on concentration
and coordination.

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:21.390
The French had more and better tanks than
the Germans, but their use was ruled by an

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:26.430
outdated doctrine: they were thinly dispersed
along the front and few had radios.

00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:31.149
Von Manstein instead had amassed 7 armoured
divisions at the spearhead of the Ardennes

00:16:31.149 --> 00:16:32.149
offensive.

00:16:32.149 --> 00:16:35.600
Guderian, in charge of three divisions, was
ordered to cross the River Meuse at Sedan,

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:39.449
where the Prussians had defeated Napoleon
III in 1870.

00:16:39.449 --> 00:16:43.410
The day of the offensive arrived: 10th of
May 1940.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.220
Everything went as planned?

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:46.220
Not really.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:50.399
The German divisions in the Low Lands, did
manage to lure and pin down the Allies, but

00:16:50.399 --> 00:16:54.439
the swift drive through the Ardennes soon
turned into a massive traffic jam.

00:16:54.439 --> 00:16:57.999
Allied aircraft could have easily destroyed
the German column, but the Luftwaffe did its

00:16:57.999 --> 00:16:59.939
job and protected them from the air.

00:16:59.939 --> 00:17:04.399
The traffic cleared and Guderian reached the
Meuse one day earlier than planned.

00:17:04.399 --> 00:17:08.330
On the 13th of May, he had infantry and engineers
cross the river in inflatable rafts, so they

00:17:08.330 --> 00:17:12.390
could neutralize French bunkers and build
pontoon bridges for the tanks.

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:14.939
By nightfall, the defenders were routed.

00:17:14.939 --> 00:17:18.179
The French counterattacked the next morning,
but Guderian, leading from the front, had

00:17:18.179 --> 00:17:21.320
made the snap decision to set up a defensive
line with artillery.

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:25.789
High command ordered him to wait for reinforcements,
but Guderian disobeyed and pushed ahead.

00:17:25.789 --> 00:17:31.159
Between May the 15th and 17th, Guderian repulsed
armoured French attacks in Stonne and Montcornet,

00:17:31.159 --> 00:17:32.970
although it was not easy.

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:38.039
Amongst his opponents, Brigadier General Charles
de Gaulle, who almost managed to capture Guderian.

00:17:38.039 --> 00:17:40.909
As he remembered:
“His tanks succeeded in penetrating to within

00:17:40.909 --> 00:17:45.531
a mile of my advanced headquarters”
This was Guderian’s masterpiece, Operation

00:17:45.531 --> 00:17:48.440
Sickle Cut was a success beyond expectations.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:53.610
This appeared too good to be true to High
Command, which may explain their later cautioun.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:58.529
By the 24th of May, the bulk of the British
Expeditionary Force was encircled at Dunkirk.

00:17:58.529 --> 00:18:00.000
An easy prey.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.490
But General Von Rundstedt, Guderian’s immediate
superior feared an allied pincer movement

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:08.690
and ordered that his panzers stopped 10 miles
from Dunkirk.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:11.570
Guderian was allowed to proceed only on the
26th.

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:15.450
By then, the French had strengthened their
defences around the Dunkirk pocket, allowing

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:17.100
for the evacuation of the British.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:18.690
This surely frustrated Guderian.

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:24.620
But, overall, his campaign was a success,
resulting in French surrender in June.

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:31.990
The Revenge of Reality
Emboldened by the victories brought on by

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:36.990
the War of Movement, Hitler started drawing
plans to invade the Soviet Union.

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:42.390
Operation Barbarossa was launched in June
1941, and Schnelle Heinz was again to the

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:43.390
fore.

00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:44.390
The campaign was nightmarish.

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:48.669
As we now know, the Army units were followed
by the SS Einsatzgruppen, specially dedicated

00:18:48.669 --> 00:18:54.100
to rounding up, deporting and murdering millions
of Jews, Roma and other targeted groups in

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:55.270
Soviet territory.

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:58.429
Guderian was leading Panzergruppe nr 2, heading
towards Moscow in late 1941.

00:18:58.429 --> 00:19:01.000
Had he been involved in such atrocities?

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:02.790
Or was he at least aware?

00:19:02.790 --> 00:19:05.120
Evidence suggests that Guderian was not an
anti-Semite.

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:10.669
Possibly he knew that Jews and other so-called
‘undesirables’ were sent to concentration

00:19:10.669 --> 00:19:12.480
camps for a life of hardships.

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:16.830
And for sure, he knew that his panzers had
been built by slave labourers from occupied

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:17.830
nations.

00:19:17.830 --> 00:19:20.919
But it may be that he didn't know about the
systematic killings.

00:19:20.919 --> 00:19:25.450
Even his wife Gretel, a stronger admirer of
Hitler’s than Guderian, later was shocked

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:27.590
by learning of the truth behind the camps.

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:31.919
Both were convinced that Himmler and the SS
were the only responsible, not Hitler.

00:19:31.919 --> 00:19:36.490
If he somehow had no knowledge of the death
squads, Guderian surely had realised by this

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:39.500
time that Hitler was not a leader he could
follow blindly.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:43.779
As the Fuhrer imposed ill-judged decisions
on his Generals, Guderian was frustrated by

00:19:43.779 --> 00:19:44.980
the lack of criticism.

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:47.080
As the Russian campaign hardened, he wrote
to his wife:

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:50.690
“Everybody is scared of the Führer and
nobody dares say anything!”

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:55.930
As Autumn and Winter set on, Guderian’s
panzers stalled in the mud and snow, harassed

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:58.159
by an increasingly stiff Russian resistance.

00:19:58.159 --> 00:20:02.651
One of his most famous quotes is
“There are no desperate situations, there

00:20:02.651 --> 00:20:04.570
are only desperate people!”

00:20:04.570 --> 00:20:07.840
And yet the situation would prove desperate
even to him.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:12.269
“We have severely underestimated the Russians,
the extent of the country and the treachery

00:20:12.269 --> 00:20:13.490
of the climate.

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:15.750
This is the revenge of reality.”

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:20.850
In December of 1941, Guderian defied an order
from Hitler to advance on Moscow, and instead

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:24.650
pulled back his Panzergruppe 100 miles from
the capital.

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:26.649
This insubordination cost him his job.

00:20:26.649 --> 00:20:30.260
Hitler forced Guderian to retire, but rewarded
him with a farm in Prussia.

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:34.390
Countryside life did not sit well with Guderian,
apparently: during this period a pre-existing

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:35.490
heart condition worsened.

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:40.070
But he had no time to properly recuperate:
he was soon to be recalled into service.

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:47.649
Back 
in action[TA3]

00:20:47.649 --> 00:20:52.640
In 1943 the situation for the Axis was worsening
and the military convinced Hitler that Guderian

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:53.640
was needed.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:57.780
Hitler agreed and recalled Guderian from retirement,
appointing him Inspector General of Panzer

00:20:57.780 --> 00:20:58.780
forces.

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:02.399
This did not appease his temper, though, and
his shouting matches with the Fuhrer resumed.

00:21:02.399 --> 00:21:07.929
In summer of 1943 he had argued to contain
Soviet counter-attacks by a strategy of dynamic

00:21:07.929 --> 00:21:08.929
mobile defence.

00:21:08.929 --> 00:21:13.190
This would imply engaging the Red Army in
a series of local clashes to defeat them in

00:21:13.190 --> 00:21:15.220
detail and delay their advance.

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:19.730
The strategy was a contradiction of his beloved
Bewegungskrieg, but it just shows how he had

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:24.279
realised that this was not a ‘silver bullet’
suitable to all theatres of war.

00:21:24.279 --> 00:21:28.799
Hitler did not listen to his advice, preferring
the proposal of General Zeitzler: concentrate

00:21:28.799 --> 00:21:34.430
almost all German armoured divisions in Kursk,
to defeat the Soviets in one decisive battle.

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:38.630
The Battle of Kursk raged on from the 4th
to the 12th of July 1943.

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:41.750
It was a close call, with heavy losses on
both sides.

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:46.230
But in the end the Soviets prevailed, and
from then on, the War on the Eastern Front

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:48.640
became one of retreats for the Germans.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:53.210
The following year, after yet another suspension
for insubordination, Guderian was approached

00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:59.190
by the circle of Colonel Von Stauffenberg,
who were plotting to assassinate Hitler.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:02.870
Guderian refused to participate, although
he did not report the conspirators, nor he

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:03.870
warned the Fuhrer.

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:08.899
The failed assassination took place on the
20th of July 1944, followed by a purge of

00:22:08.899 --> 00:22:10.030
military leadership.

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:13.690
Ironically, now Guderian was one of the few
remaining leaders that Hitler could trust

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:14.690
for the war effort.

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:18.480
The Fuhrer appointed Guderian as new Army
Chief of Staff.

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:23.690
Guderian's main focus now was to defend Germany
from the impending Soviet counter-attack and

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:24.690
occupation.

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:28.519
He knew very well that the war could not be
won but intended to resist as long as possible.

00:22:28.519 --> 00:22:33.360
The main hindrance to his plans, was the meddling
Fuhrer himself.

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:41.470
The quick one finally stops
In March 1945, he was vocally opposing Hitler

00:22:41.470 --> 00:22:42.630
in Staff meetings.

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:46.510
Hitler decided to get rid of Guderian and
ordered him to take six weeks of sick leave.

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:50.790
The order was meant to hurt him, ironically
it may have saved him.

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:54.409
Berlin would be surrounded by the Red Army
the following month, while Heinz and Gretel

00:22:54.409 --> 00:22:56.769
were in a cardiology hospital in Munich.

00:22:56.769 --> 00:23:01.140
This is where US forces captured Guderian
on the 10th of May 1945.

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:04.519
The fifth anniversary of Operation Sickle
Cut.

00:23:04.519 --> 00:23:07.740
Guderian was sent to Nuremberg, but not put
immediately on trial.

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:11.860
The Russians wanted to charge him with war
crimes, but the Western Allies didn't comply.

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:16.630
In 1946, Guderian was imprisoned in the POW
camps of Allendorf and Neustadt.

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:20.050
During his captivity, Allied military tried
to evaluate the extent of his involvement

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:21.350
in Nazi war crimes.

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:26.270
As he did not cooperate in interrogations,
they placed him in confinement with two other

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:29.570
Generals, Geyr and Leeb, and wiretapped the
cell.

00:23:29.570 --> 00:23:32.529
Even them he kept his cards close to his chest.

00:23:32.529 --> 00:23:36.309
This is the only conversation in which he
admits his support of party policies:

00:23:36.309 --> 00:23:41.090
Geyr: "Any objective observer will admit that
National Socialism raised the social status

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:45.970
of the worker, and in some respects even his
standard of living as long as that was possible."

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:49.690
Leeb: "This is one of the great achievements
of National Socialism.

00:23:49.690 --> 00:23:54.570
The excesses of National Socialism were in
the first and final analysis due to the warped

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:58.649
personality of the Fuhrer"
Guderian: "The fundamental principles were

00:23:58.649 --> 00:24:01.170
fine."[TA4]
It was not enough to charge him and Guderian

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:04.460
was released on his 60th birthday
[7th of June 1948]

00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:09.380
In the following years he contributed to the
organisation of the Bundeswehr, the armed

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:10.630
forces of West Germany.

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:15.059
He returned to writing, drafting his memoirs
and more military theory works.

00:24:15.059 --> 00:24:19.029
His autobiography ‘Panzer Leader’, also
known as ‘Memoirs of a soldier’ was published

00:24:19.029 --> 00:24:21.840
in 1952, becoming an international best seller.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:24.630
Soon after its publication, Guderian's health
failed.

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:30.590
He died in Schwangau bei Fussen on the 14th
of May, 1954, the anniversary of his victory

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:32.620
on the Meuse.

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:39.850
A critical look[TA5]
So far I have told you the most widely accepted

00:24:39.850 --> 00:24:45.130
version of Guderian’s life story, the official
version for many decades after his death.

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:49.470
However in the year 2000s, Guderian’s career
and legacy have come under scrutiny from several

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:54.299
historians, such as Russell Hart, Marcel Stein
or Pier Paolo Battistelli.

00:24:54.299 --> 00:24:58.029
These authors claim that Guderian’s military
capabilities were undeniable, although he

00:24:58.029 --> 00:25:00.330
greatly exaggerated them through his writings.

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:03.940
Hart for example claims that his victories
always came from a position of strength and

00:25:03.940 --> 00:25:07.080
numerical superiority, never from a position
of weakness.

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:11.360
While Battistelli argues that his true genius
was as author and publicist of his own image

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.679
as the mastermind behind the blitzkrieg, or
bewegungskrieg.

00:25:13.679 --> 00:25:17.201
It can be argued that being in a position
of strength is a sign of skill, too, as it

00:25:17.201 --> 00:25:19.110
requires planning and preparation.

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:22.780
And that autobiographies naturally tend to
be embellished accounts.

00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:25.169
Other arguments are more disturbing, though.

00:25:25.169 --> 00:25:30.909
Guderian always portrayed himself as distanced
from Nazi excesses, and as a fair combatant.

00:25:30.909 --> 00:25:35.831
Yet, Marcel Stein writes that Guderian’s
units in Russia actively executed under his

00:25:35.831 --> 00:25:38.539
orders the infamous ‘Commissar Orders’.

00:25:38.539 --> 00:25:43.760
This was the summary execution of war prisoners
suspected to be Political Commissars or actively

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:45.730
representing Bolshevik ideology.

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:50.430
And that, is a war crime that would not have
been forgiven at the Nuremberg trials.

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:55.220
Hart also claims that during the July 1944
coup against Hitler, Guderian opportunistically

00:25:55.220 --> 00:26:00.260
played both sides, ready to join the winner
to get a promotion as Chief of Staff.

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:05.039
As acting Chief of Staff, on the 29th of July,
Guderian issued an order forcing all officers

00:26:05.039 --> 00:26:10.059
to become members of the Nazi Party, implying
that he was closer to Nazi ideology that he

00:26:10.059 --> 00:26:11.059
would have us believe.

00:26:11.059 --> 00:26:20.190
I will leave the final judgement to you, in
the comments.

00:26:20.190 --> 00:26:31.570
Heinz Guderian: a military genius loyal to
his Country, or a self-serving opportunistic

00:26:31.570 --> 00:26:33.750
Nazi?

